<!--
  Device HAL manifest (VINTF) for the Meizu M2 Note on LineageOS 20.0.

  Why this file exists.  Without a DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE there is no
  /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml and hwservicemanager answers getTransport()
  with EMPTY for every vendor HAL.  Most clients survive that: system/libhidl's
  getRawServiceInternal() only warns on an undeclared interface and still tries
  hwbinder, then passthrough.

  Wi-Fi is the exception.  frameworks/opt/net/wifi HalDeviceManager decides
  whether a vendor HAL exists at all by asking the manifest directly:

      isSupportedInternal():
          IServiceManager.getTransport(IWifi.kInterfaceName, "default")
              != Transport.EMPTY

  With no manifest that returns EMPTY, WifiNative logs "Vendor Hal not
  supported, ignoring start", IWifi.start() is never called, and
  wifi_load_driver() never writes "1" into /dev/wmtWifi.  wlan0 is then never
  created.

  FACT specific to this handset: Wi-Fi DOES work on it.  The 2026-06-18 capture
  v194_15.1_ais_full_scan shows "wlan0" in identity.txt alongside
  sys.boot_completed=1, plus a full dumpsys wifi and a successful scan.  So the
  two entries below describe a path that has been observed live here — unlike
  the m5s tree, where the same two entries are still a hypothesis.

  FACT on the other side: the 2026-07-06 subsystem matrix recorded TRB-FULL as
  "error" with the cause "нет /vendor/etc/vintf/manifest.xml".  This file is the
  direct answer to that finding.

  Scope is deliberately narrow.  A HAL declared here but not actually served
  hangs its client (the m681 light@2.0 lesson).  The LOS 15.1 tree for this
  handset ships a 7.3 KB manifest listing a dozen HALs; that set is NOT copied,
  because on this port none of those services is wired yet (see the
  "NOT WIRED YET" block in device.mk).  Add an entry only together with runtime
  proof that the service answers.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device">
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.2</version>
        <interface>
            <name>IWifi</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
    <hal format="hidl">
        <name>android.hardware.wifi.supplicant</name>
        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
        <version>1.1</version>
        <interface>
            <name>ISupplicant</name>
            <instance>default</instance>
        </interface>
    </hal>
</manifest>
